ould tell: this one wasn't going to run.\n\nThe whispers became a voice. It knew Ben Marsh's name. It offered a deal: leave the marsh village, leave a child's drawing dated tomorrow, and walk away clean. Ben Marsh considered it for exactly as long as it took to be afraid — then refused."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Night', 'text': "the medium Madame Zara arrived — of course the medium Madame Zara did, called by the thing friends are — with headlights and a crowbar and no plan at all. Together they forced the Hollow Man back toward the nursery, toward a child's drawing dated tomorrow, toward the broken place.\n\nBen Marsh understood then what had to be done: not destroy a child's drawing dated tomorrow, but complete it. Join the halves. Speak the name. Give the Hollow Man what it had wanted all along — to be seen, to be mourned, to be released.\n\nIt came on the anniversary, as Ben Marsh knew it would. The fog pressed against the windows of the marsh village like a face. a child's drawing dated tomorrow activated — played, filled, showed, rang, drew, unlocked — and the Hollow Man stepped out of the walls."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Aftermath', 'text': "Madame Zara sent a note: 'You did what I couldn't. The house is clean.' Ben Marsh framed it. Not from pride — from proof. Proof that the dark can be answered.\n\nthe marsh village is for sale again, and Ben Marsh is staying. Someone has to be here who knows. Someone has to keep the light on. That someone is Ben Marsh now.\n\nThe archives got a new entry, in Ben Marsh's handwriting: what happened, what worked, what it cost. Let the next tenant know. Let no one ever pretend again."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue: The Light Kept', 'text': "the medium Madame Zara visits every anniversary with pancakes and bad jokes. They don't talk about that night. They don't need to. It's in the walls — but the walls, now, are just walls.\n\nYears later, Ben Marsh still walks the marsh village at night, checking the rooms the way you check on sleeping children. The nursery is a study now. The cold spot is just a draft. Mostly.\n\nSometimes a child's drawing dated tomorrow catches the light oddly, and Ben Marsh feels the old chill. Then Ben Marsh touches it, whole and quiet, and remembers the choice: to face it, to mend it, to stay."}]}

